# Kubernetes safety

## Read-only default

When triage or manifest review is authorized, these are fine without a mutate
verb: list/get/log/events/top/stats/`configuration_view`.

Do **not** do any of the following unless the user gave an explicit verb this
turn naming the object (or clearly scoped set):

- delete pods, namespaces, workloads, PVCs, or CRDs
- scale replicas (including scale-to-zero)
- exec / attach / port-forward into containers
- apply or patch live cluster state
- create privileged Pods, hostNetwork, or hostPath for "convenience"
- edit RBAC that widens cluster-admin or bind wildcards

This MCP surface is **read-heavy** (list/get/log/events). If a mutate must
happen, use the project's normal apply path (kubectl/GitOps) only after the
user authorized it — do not invent write tools that are not connected.

## Irreversible — confirm blast radius

Before proceeding when clearly authorized:

| Action | State risk |
| --- | --- |
| Namespace delete | All namespaced objects in that ns |
| PVC / PV delete | Persistent data loss |
| Production scale-to-zero | Immediate outage |
| Delete Deployment/StatefulSet | Workload + possible volume claims |

State the object, namespace, and consequence in one sentence, then act only if
authorization already covers that irreversible loss.

## Manifest apply risk

Editing YAML in-repo is craft (`kubernetes-manifests`). Applying it to a live
cluster is a separate mutate — requires an explicit apply/deploy verb and
follows this rule. Prefer dry-run / server-side diff when the toolchain
supports it.

## Defaults

1. Prefer the smallest mutate that satisfies the ask.
2. Never "fix prod" by deleting random Pending pods without naming them.
3. Do not paste kubeconfig credentials or tokens into the reply.
4. Site production cluster names stay unresolved until listed from context.
